From 4acad3532d7b42f005f3d1564fc1df21cdf4ae0d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: L0garithmic <L0garithmic@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 7 Sep 2026 13:21:45 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] indexeddb: implement IDBCursor continue, continuePrimaryKey
 and advance

The cursor could be opened and its first record read, and then it could never
move: continue/advance/continuePrimaryKey were commented out of IDBCursor.webidl
and absent from the binding. Any bulk read over an object store or index stopped
after one record, which is fatal for an app that syncs history.

The iteration algorithm itself was already here and correct (iterate_cursor), and
execute_async already accepted an existing request, which is exactly what the
spec's "iterate a cursor" needs, since a cursor reuses one IDBRequest for every
step. So this wires those together and adds the per-method preconditions:
transaction active, source not deleted, got-value flag set, a continue key that
actually moves the cursor in its own direction, continuePrimaryKey restricted to
an index source with direction next or prev, and advance(0) throwing TypeError.

Verified at runtime against the store: a five-record walk with continue() visits
all five in order and terminates; advance(2) visits 1, 3, 5; continue(4) jumps to
4 and then 5; and calling continue() twice without waiting throws
InvalidStateError as the spec requires.

update() and delete() on a cursor remain unimplemented.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>
